  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA strengthening low level jet ahead of a mid level shortwave was\nleading to widespread showers. Favorable moisture advection and PWATs\nabove 1.6 inches will lead to moderate to heavy rain at times. Can\nnot rule out localized flooding. \n\nArea rain gages indicate rainfall up to inch with the highest totals\nacross ILN/s southern counties. Shower activity will continue thru \nthis afternoon before beginning to taper off from west to east late \nthis afternoon into this evening. Rainfall totals are expected to \nrange from near a half inch northwest to 1.5 inches south. \nInstability will remain very weak with only a thin ribbon expected \nlate in the day across the west ahead of the front. Therefore, any \nembedded thunderstorm activity should be very limited. After the rain\nends - a period of drizzle will be possible this evening with clouds\nlingering into the overnight hours. \n\n\n\nKey message 2) \n\nMid level trof to drop down across the Great Lakes region and \nthrough the upper Ohio Valley Friday with a low closing off Friday \nnight into Saturday. This system will bring a chance of showers \nFriday into Friday night. A secondary frontal passage will \nalso usher in a somewhat cooler airmass heading into the weekend. \nDaytime highs will only be in the 50s Saturday and Sunday. \nTemperatures will then gradually rebound into the 60s Monday into \nTuesday.",
